s other folks with specific fitness  or sports goals may need to meet certain  macronutrient and calorie intake goals  to fuel their activity in these cases  having go-to meals or a general meal  plan might help them to fuel and recover  with more ease and less mental load we  also know that structure and consistency  with meals can be beneficial for folks  who are neurodivergent or who have adhd  as it helps to make functioning more  manageable by allowing the brain to  focus on one thing at a time without  getting overstimulated or overwhelmed by  choice so when the intention is to  actually free up mental space and  relieve you of the burden of thinking  about food all day long while still  nourishing yourself it actually can  possibly help to support food freedom on  the other hand we often see that folks  who struggle with eating disorders or  disordered eating will have specific  safe foods or meals that they stick to  every single day and rarely deviate from  this this may be because they know the  exact amount of calories that they're  getting in that meal and that that  precise amount of calories or macros  doesn't seem to cause weight gain in  folks with more orthorexic tendencies  they may perceive a particular food to  be clean or specifically healthy and may  be unwilling to test the boundaries to  collect body data on how a new food  makes them feel out of fear that they  may trigger an adverse sensation or  effect regardless of the unique  rationale folks who struggle with their  relationship with food and experience a  lot of food fear may intentionally eat  the same thing every day as a way to  feel safe and in control around food  ironically this actually tends to have  the opposite effect over time and it  serves to really reinforce our fear  around foods outside of our routine so  this is where we need to ask ourselves  about flexibility can your weekends look  different from your weekdays are some of  your meals more varied than others are  you 